WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
Hoops!

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.000
On this playground, it's a whole new game!

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:26.380
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.100
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.440
And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.560
Guys, I got the feeling that basketball for us is the sport that, you know, of the major

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.700
sports, the one that we know the least about.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.940
Is anybody here a basketball fan?

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:47.080
I mean, I played basketball in my youth, like, before high school.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.140
And I mean, I don't really watch it as much as I would, like, football or hockey, but I

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.080
still enjoy basketball.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.880
Yeah, I wasn't, you know, I didn't play many sports.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.300
I mean, I played baseball when I was younger.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.180
But, like, you know, I was aware of all, you know, I played casually with friends, all these,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.660
you know, football and baseball and hockey and stuff.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.500
But basketball was the one that I just never, never really played.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.880
So whenever I did, it was, it wasn't pretty.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.780
Sean, you bring up a good point, though.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.820
And that's actually what I was leading on to is, like, you know, even if we don't, none

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.300
of us really follow the NBA or care about basketball all that much.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.640
Everybody, like, every kid at one point or another, like, goes to a basketball court or their friend

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.840
has a basketball hoop in their front yard or whatever.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:44.160
And they play probably a more faithful version of what hoops is going for today than, you

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.120
know, the double dribble we got earlier in the NES's lifespan, which was more of like,

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.360
you know, a two-on-two NBA jam slash, you know, NBA style game.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.400
Hoops is trying to go for that street basketball that I'm sure we're all just a little more

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.320
familiar with. Oh, yeah.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.820
They've got, it's all half court.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.640
You can only do as many as two-on-two.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:13.320
They even have some of the common ways to decide who gets first possession of just, you

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.880
know, shooting for possession.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.720
That's what we did on the playground.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:24.280
Um, uh, it's actually pretty, pretty faithful to, uh, the kids.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.820
Yeah. It feels more like, I mean, it's faithful in the sense that it's, you know, it's the way

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.740
that we played probably if you're just, if you're just casually playing basketball, but

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:38.900
also in the sense that in a, in a game like, uh, double dribble, there's all these other

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.660
players. It's kind of like a lot of these other sports games that feel like there's, there's some

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.900
simulation going on while you're playing too.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.900
And there's a little bit of a disconnect where this is like, if you're playing one-on-one,

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.780
I mean, you're in control the whole time.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:57.740
Um, so I think that that makes it also a little more quickly translatable.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.040
Yeah. I guess the one thing that it really doesn't, uh, that is very unrealistic is there's some

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.420
unseen, uh, uh, referee that's calling you for charging every few minutes.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.940
Yeah. You guys didn't have the local playground referee who made all the calls?

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.180
No, we did not.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:15.400
No, of course not.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.300
No, I, I actually, uh, think that when I was playing basketball as a kid, I, I felt like

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.280
you could just travel too.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.620
Like there was almost no rules, uh, which seems impossible.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.040
Well, then you're just an asshole, you know?

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.020
Yeah. You're just an asshole. Yeah.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.980
But that's like, that's kind of how recess rules were, right?

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.000
Like everybody just kind of makes up the rules as they go.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.260
I, I mean, I, I would call people out for traveling, but that's just me.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.220
I feel like you're a stickler.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.260
We were pretty like self-governing in that sense where it's like someone might try and get

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.360
away with that, but everyone's going to call you on it.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:50.060
Yeah. If this game had, instead of like a pop-up that says traveling or a pop-up that says

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:56.660
charging, um, if it simulated an argument after that and like the game kind of kept going,

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:03.320
but there was just a lot of like back and forth, um, then it would be much more, uh, authentic.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.100
Yeah. I think what they should have done is, is maybe, maybe you hit, you hit select to, to like

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.120
call someone and say that they were traveling and then they have to agree with you before

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.240
the game could move on.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.680
So you have to get into a real life argument about it.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:19.280
Yeah. There's a speech mini game.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.540
Right. It's a, it's a house rules mode where the rules are just kind of decided as they go about,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.460
you know, just talk it out with the computer.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.980
It's like, all right, we're going to agree to do this, but not this.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.040
We're going to points are now worth four.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.780
Uh, cool.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.580
So, you know, the one thing that, uh, hoops, uh, doesn't have going for it, but apparently,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.200
you know, lots of sports games back then couldn't get it is any kind of recognizable names.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.460
Instead, they just try to tease you with the idea that the legends of the playground are all

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:51.600
here. The legends of the playground are all here.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:57.380
Mr. Doc, Jammer, Legs, Bomber, Wiz, eight players in all.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.540
I mean, if you're talking about the legends of the playground, you should list them all

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.640
out, right? Like, I mean, how much, how much better could the other four be if you're not even going

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.980
to mention them by name?

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:14.060
I mean, well, I, are you arguing that, um, uh, that, that they should be legends that

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:15.220
we personally know?

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:21.400
So I'm just saying why even go that far to give them like, you know, I, I guess like

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.020
I could see the argument of it shouldn't be like backyard basketball where it's just like

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.240
kid versions of the NBA stars.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:35.020
But, uh, you know, I think like teasing us with that, the legends of the playground and

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.920
it's filled with names of people we just never met or knew.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.540
I don't know. It doesn't mean, it doesn't add any emphasis.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:45.240
Yeah. Has the legends of this particular city's two playgrounds.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.000
That's true. Cause they do say on this playground.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.120
Yeah. A whole new game.

